# Castivio — the local settings that are never committed.
#
# Copy this file to `local.properties` in this same directory and fill in the values you
# have. That file is in `.gitignore` and must stay there; nothing in it ever reaches the
# repository, a build artefact that is published, or a log.
#
#     cp local.properties.example local.properties
#
# **On the machine that builds, and never through github.com.** The web interface commits
# what it is given without consulting `.gitignore`, so a file created there is a file in the
# repository however thoroughly it is excluded — which is how a set of real credentials once
# reached this project's history and had to be replaced. `.gitignore` protects `git add`. It
# cannot protect a file written straight onto the remote.
#
# Every value below is optional. A clone with none of them compiles, runs, and passes its
# tests — the features that need one report themselves as not set up rather than failing.
# Android Studio writes `sdk.dir` into `local.properties` by itself the first time it opens
# the project, so an existing file should be added to rather than replaced.
#
# On CI there is no file to write, so the same three names are read from the environment
# instead. `Secrets.kt` in buildSrc does both and prefers the file.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenSubtitles — for "find subtitles online" in the player.
#
# Two separate things are needed, and the first alone is not enough:
#
#   1. An API key. opensubtitles.com → log in → Consumers → New Consumer. Free.
#   2. An ordinary account's username and password. The v1 API will search with the key
#      alone, but a *download* link is only issued to a session, and a session comes from
#      POST /api/v1/login with those credentials. This is how OpenSubtitles counts a
#      person's daily downloads, so there is no way round it and no reason to want one.
#
# Leave them empty and the player keeps every subtitle a file already carries; only the
# search is switched off, and it says so.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

OPENSUBTITLES_API_KEY=
OPENSUBTITLES_USERNAME=
OPENSUBTITLES_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Building with them
#
#   ./gradlew assembleDebug
#
# and install app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk. Android Studio's Run does the same
# thing. Gradle tracks this file, so editing the three lines above invalidates the build
# that read them and the next one picks the new values up — there is nothing to clean.
#
# CI builds the same APK from the same three names held as encrypted repository secrets
# (Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions), because the APKs this project installs come
# from CI rather than from a developer's machine. That is a deliberate trade: a credential
# compiled into a downloadable artefact can be read back out of it by anyone who has the
# file, so the account behind these three values should be one kept for this purpose and
# rotated the moment it is shared further than intended.
#
# To check which you are holding, open the player's subtitle sheet: "Find subtitles online"
# says it is not set up when the three values were empty at build time.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
